  "raw": {
    "https://fa-exad-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/343": {
      "title": "Nicolais School of Business - Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Category: FACULTY JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Staten Island, NY, United States The Nicolais School of Business at Wagner College, located on Staten Island in New York City, invites applicants to apply for a full-time, tenure-track, position for an Assistant Professor of Management to start in Spring 2027. The ideal candidate will help develop the management skills business students require upon graduation; be current with pedagogy including online and hybrid learning modalities; possess an ability to innovate in the classroom through experiential learning, real-world applications, and industry partnerships; possess the desire to participate in the growth of the school. The Nicolais School of Business at Wagner College, located on Staten Island in New York City, invites applicants to apply for a full-time, tenure-track, position for an Assistant Professor of Management to start in Spring 2027. The ideal candidate will help develop the management skills business students require upon graduation; be current with pedagogy including online and hybrid learning modalities; possess an ability to innovate in the classroom through experiential learning, real-world applications, and industry partnerships; possess the desire to participate in the growth of the school. About the Nicolais School of Business In 2016, Wagner College established the Nicolais School of Business (NSB), to honor two of the college's stalwart supporters of business education, the late Michael A. Nicolais '49 H'14 and his wife, Margaret Christie Nicolais '49 H'14. The Nicolais School of Business enrolls almost 400 undergraduate majors and 75 MBA students with ambitious goals to grow enrollment at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The NSB offers four undergraduate majors in accounting, finance, marketing, and management, a BA in economics and an MBA with concentrations in management, marketing, and finance. Students are at the core of our decisions. Through practical education, based on a liberal arts tradition, our mission is to prepare students to be career-ready, data-informed, ethical business leaders, effective communicators, and responsible global citizens. We are making investments to develop innovative, relevant, and high-impact curriculum and programs to prepare students to thrive in an ever-changing business world, and to cultivate and forge community and industry partnerships and to establish our brand identity. The Nicolais School of Business has the full support of the senior leadership of the College, an active Board of Trustees, and an engaged alumni base. The NSB is accredited by Accreditation Council for Business Schools and Programs (ACBSP). NSB is also a member of the Association to Advance Collegiate Schools of Business (AACSB). Given our location, there are many opportunities to build upon existing partnerships and form new collaborations to help develop faculty members and to identify and provide opportunities for our students. We seek a candidate who is committed to growth and who wants to be a part of this vision. Qualifications The candidate must possess a doctoral degree (or ABD near completion). Prior teaching experience is a plus, but not required. The candidate must possess a robust interest in excellent teaching, demonstrate an interest in research, and a strong commitment to college and school-wide service activities such as serving on committees and advising undergraduate and graduate students. We are seeking individuals who are passionate about teaching student success and researching a variety of topics. Responsibilities Teaching -The standard teaching load at the Nicolais School of Business is three sections per semester (six per academic year). Student advising at the undergraduate and graduate level. Participation in College-wide and school-specific committees. Publishing research in peer-reviewed journals. Presenting research at academic and industry conferences. Application Materials Curriculum Vitae. Cover letter highlighting the candidate's ability to meet the position qualifications and highlighting relevant aspects of (a) teaching, (b) research, and (C) your specific interest in the Nicolais School of Business and Wagner College. A statement demonstrating the candidate's commitment to their teaching, research, professional service, and the impact on business education. Two letters of recommendation. Please send the application materials to: Michael J. Driscoll, Ed.D. Dean, Nicolais School of Business nsb.faculty.search@wagner.edu For full consideration, applications should be received by October 15th, 2026. Skills: Located in an increasingly diverse metropolitan area, Wagner College is committed to scholarship and community outreach relevant to the needs of New York City. Wagner values campus diversity (domestic and international) and in keeping with this initiative, it welcomes applications from diverse candidates and candidates who support diversity and internationalization efforts. "
    },
    "https://fa-exad-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/341": {
      "title": "Evelyn L. Sprio School of Nursing - Associated Clinical Faculty of Practice",
      "description": "Category: FACULTY JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Staten Island, NY, United States The Evelyn L. Spiro School of Nursing at Wagner College, a National League of Nursing Center of Excellence, and ACEN accredited seeks an individual at the Associated Clinical Professor of Practice level(full-time) for a position beginning in Spring 2027. Candidates must have current RN and FNP licenses in New York State and (preferably) a terminal degree, Ph.D., DNP or Ed.D or enrolled in a program, with a background in curriculum and teaching/clinical expertise. The candidate will be expected to teach within the undergraduate nursing program and graduate programs and participate in faculty meetings and student advisement. Associated Clinical Professor of Practice The Evelyn L. Spiro School of Nursing The Evelyn L. Spiro School of Nursing at Wagner College, a National League of Nursing Center of Excellence, and ACEN accredited seeks an individual at the Associated Clinical Professor of Practice level(full-time) for a position beginning in Spring 2027. Candidates must have current RN and FNP licenses in New York State and (preferably) a terminal degree, Ph.D., DNP or Ed.D or enrolled in a program, with a background in curriculum and teaching/clinical expertise. The candidate will be expected to teach within the undergraduate nursing program and graduate programs and participate in faculty meetings and student advisement. Interested candidates should send a cover letter, curriculum vitae, transcripts, statement of teaching philosophy and research interests, along with names, addresses, and phone numbers of two references to: Dr. Patricia Tooker Dean, Professor Evelyn L. Spiro School of Nursing Wagner College One Campus Road Staten Island, NY 10301 For e-mail inquiries : Patricia Mustacchio, Administrative Assistant at patricia.mustacchio @wagner.edu The application review process will be open until filled. Located in an increasingly diverse metropolitan area, Wagner College is committed to scholarship and community outreach relevant to the needs of New York City. Wagner values campus diversity (domestic and international) and in keeping with this initiative, welcomes applications from diverse candidates and candidates who support diversity and internationalization efforts. "
    },
    "https://fa-exad-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/345": {
      "title": "Social and Behavioral Sciences - Psychology Department - Professor",
      "description": "Category: FACULTY JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Staten Island, NY, United States Tenure-track Faculty Position in Cognitive Psychology The Department of Psychology at Wagner College invites applications for an open-rank faculty position in the area of Cognitive Psychology to begin August 2027. Candidates should hold a Ph.D. at the time of appointment. Rank will be dependent upon the candidate’s experience. In addition to undergraduate courses in cognitive psychology and research area, the successful candidate should be able to teach statistics and methodology. Candidates who can involve undergraduate students in laboratory research are highly desirable. Additionally, candidates with an interest in teaching one or more of the following will be given particular attention: history of psychology, sport psychology, and sensation and perception. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching, student mentoring, inclusive pedagogy, and scholarly research. The teaching load is 3/3, with opportunities to teach summer courses. Wagner College, located in Staten Island, NY, values diversity, equity, and inclusion and encourages applications from candidates whose experiences and perspectives will contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Applicants should submit the following as a single PDF file under the \"Resume\" button: Letter of application Curriculum vitae Graduate transcript Statement of teaching philosophy and experience Evidence of teaching excellence (if available) Statement of current and future research plans Contact information for three professional references Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to: Dr. Laurence Nolan Interim Division Chair, Social & Behavioral Sciences Wagner College LNolan@wagner.edu "
    },
    "https://fa-exad-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/346": {
      "title": "Social and Behavioral Sciences - Psychology Department - Professor",
      "description": "Category: FACULTY JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Staten Island, NY, United States Tenure-track faculty position in Human Services: Criminal Justice Wagner College's Division of Social & Behavioral Sciences invites applications for an open-rank, tenure-track faculty position in Criminal Justice beginning in August 2027. Applicants must possess a Ph.D. in Criminal Justice or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Rank will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The successful candidate will play an integral role in the continued growth of Wagner College's Human Services major by supporting its Criminal Justice concentration through teaching, advising, recruitment, and curriculum development. Candidates should be prepared to teach undergraduate courses including The Criminal Justice System, Criminal Procedure, Criminal Law, Criminology, Law and Society, Introduction to Sociology , and other courses consistent with their expertise and the needs of the department. Preference will be given to candidates with expertise in criminal justice, criminology, criminal law, criminal procedure, law and society, or related areas . Additional teaching responsibilities may be assigned based on the successful candidate's expertise and departmental needs. In addition to teaching, the faculty member will advise and mentor undergraduate students, contribute to student recruitment and the continued growth of the Human Services major, maintain an active program of scholarship, and participate in departmental, divisional, and college service. Candidates holding a J.D. in addition to a Ph.D. are especially encouraged to apply. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching, student mentoring, inclusive pedagogy, and scholarly research. The teaching load is 3/3, with opportunities to teach summer courses. Wagner College, located in Staten Island, NY, values diversity, equity, and inclusion and encourages applications from candidates whose experiences and perspectives will contribute to the excellence of our academic community. Application Materials Applicants should submit the following as a single PDF file under the \"Resume\" button: Letter of application Curriculum vitae Graduate transcript Statement of teaching philosophy and experience Evidence of teaching excellence (if available) Statement of current and future research plans Contact information for three professional references Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to: Dr. Laurence Nolan Interim Division Chair, Social & Behavioral Sciences Wagner College LNolan@wagner.edu "
    },
    "https://fa-exad-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/334": {
      "title": "Humanities - History - Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Category: FACULTY JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Staten Island, NY, United States The Department of History at Wagner College invites applications for adjunct instructors to teach undergraduate courses Adjunct Professor – Part Time Department: History The Department of History at Wagner College invites applications for adjunct instructors to teach undergraduate courses in one or more of the following areas: Global History African American History (1865–Present) New York City History History of Science and Medicine in America Successful candidates will demonstrate a commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching and the ability to engage students in a dynamic learning environment. Qualifications Master's degree in History or a closely related field required. Prior college-level teaching experience is preferred. Application Instructions To apply, please submit a brief letter of application and a current curriculum vitae (CV) via email to: Sarah Donovan sdonovan@wagner.edu "
    },
    "https://fa-exad-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/269": {
      "title": "Physician Assistant Program - Research Coordinator",
      "description": "Category: FACULTY JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Staten Island, NY, United States PA Program Research Coordinator Full Time (32 hours/week at PA Program & 8 hours/week for Clinical Practice/Research) PURPOSE: Coordinate the Research Components of the Didactic, Clinical, and Advanced Clinical Phase of the PA Program. DUTIES/RESPONSIBILITIES: Responsible for coordination of student development of thesis projects and research work. Prepares syllabi and learning objective for all research related courses. Provides analysis of research related courses for appropriateness of structure, sequence, and content. Schedules classes, seminar sessions, research meetings, poster presentations, and thesis defense, focusing on a sequenced, building block approach. Prepares and/or reviews assessment tools, collaborating with faculty in the construction, analysis, and outcomes of assessment tools. Responsible for workload equivalent of three courses per semester. Serves as a student advisor, monitoring student progress, meeting with students, and assuring the implementation of appropriate interventions, when required. Analyzes student and curriculum outcomes for review at committee meetings and by the Executive Program Director. Provides analysis of student evaluations of courses, learning experiences, and teaching instructors for review at committee meetings and by the Executive Director. Participates in clinical site visits, as assigned. Actively participates in the remediation process of students, when required. Serves as an advisor for research thesis groups. Attends and participates in PA committee meetings, program activities, and campus events. Serves as a Chair of a Committee, developing agenda, preparing minutes, and providing outcome reports. Contributes to ongoing self-study process and formal reports to ARC-PA. Serves as a faculty leader for experiential service learning events (e.g.: Medical Education/Mission Trips) Attends professional conferences representing the PA Program and the College. QUALIFICATIONS: Work Experience: Minimum 5 years of academic and/or clinical experience Education: Master’s degree (MS, MPA, MPH) Doctorate Preferred (PhD, DHSC, DMS, EdD) SPECIAL CONSIDERATIONS: 1 day a week dedicated to Clinical Practice or Research. Available weekends for campus events (e.g.: Campus Visit Days) Available evenings for program events (e.g.: Research Forums) "
    }
